battclock.resource/ReadBattClock              battclock.resource/ReadBattClock

   NAME
	ReadBattClock -- The date the clock says, in seconds since 1 January 1978.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn ReadBattClock(cb: *BattClockBase) u32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -8.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	The seconds since 1 January 1978, 00:00:00; 0 when the clock held no
	date, or there is no clock.

   BEHAVIOR
	Reads the board's clock chip, asks the Raspberry Pi's firmware for
	its RTC, or works the date out from the chip's RTC timer. A clock that
	holds no date - a chip whose cell ran low or whose registers hold no
	date, a Raspberry Pi's RTC before 1978 after a power-on without a
	cell, an RTC timer after a power-on - is put in working order and set
	to 1 January 1978, as ResetBattClock does, and the answer is 0.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, for the resource's semaphore and for i2c.device; for
	  the Raspberry Pi's firmware it spins, a millisecond or so.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: the resource's semaphore, for the call.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing changes hands.

   NOTES
	The resource sets the system time from this at its start; afterwards
	the system time is timer.device's, and the two part ways as the
	clocks drift. `C:SetClock SAVE` brings the clock back to the system
	time. The date is whatever the system time was when it was written:
	local time, where a zone is set.

   BUGS
	The RTC timer's slow clock is an RC oscillator, measured at each start:
	over a long run without a write the date it gives after a reset can
	be off by some seconds a day.

   SEE ALSO
	`WriteBattClock`, `ResetBattClock`, utility.library/Amiga2Date,
	C:SetClock

   EXAMPLES
	    var date: utility.ClockData = .{};
	    ub.Amiga2Date(cb.ReadBattClock(), &date);
battclock.resource/ResetBattClock            battclock.resource/ResetBattClock

   NAME
	ResetBattClock -- The clock put in working order, its date 1 January 1978, 00:00:00.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn ResetBattClock(cb: *BattClockBase) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -4.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	None.

   BEHAVIOR
	A clock chip has its control registers cleared - running, no test
	mode, no alarm or timer interrupt - and its date set; the Raspberry
	Pi's RTC has its date set. The chip's RTC timer has its reckoning
	set, so that it holds a date again. Without a clock nothing happens.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, for the resource's semaphore and for i2c.device; for
	  the Raspberry Pi's firmware it spins, a millisecond or so.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: the resource's semaphore, for the call.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing changes hands.

   NOTES
	The system time is not changed: `C:SetClock LOAD` sets it from the
	clock.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`ReadBattClock`, `WriteBattClock`, C:SetClock

   EXAMPLES
	    const cb: *BattClockBase = @ptrCast(sys.OpenResource(battclock.BATTCLOCKNAME) orelse return);
	    cb.ResetBattClock();
battclock.resource/WriteBattClock            battclock.resource/WriteBattClock

   NAME
	WriteBattClock -- The clock set to a date.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn WriteBattClock(cb: *BattClockBase, seconds: u32) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -12.

   INPUTS
	- `seconds` - the date, in seconds since 1 January 1978, 00:00:00.

   RESULT
	None.

   BEHAVIOR
	Writes the date to the board's clock chip, has the Raspberry Pi's
	firmware set its RTC to it, or makes the chip's RTC timer's reckoning
	give it from now on. Without a clock nothing happens.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, for the resource's semaphore and for i2c.device; for
	  the Raspberry Pi's firmware it spins, a millisecond or so.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: the resource's semaphore, for the call.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing changes hands.

   NOTES
	The system time is not changed. `C:SetClock SAVE` writes the system
	time here.

   BUGS
	A PCF8563 keeps the years to 2099; a later date is kept as the last
	second of 2099.

   SEE ALSO
	`ReadBattClock`, `ResetBattClock`, utility.library/Date2Amiga,
	C:SetClock

   EXAMPLES
	    var now: timer.TimeVal = .{};
	    timer_base.GetSysTime(&now);
	    cb.WriteBattClock(now.secs);
